WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.220
You pay your gym today for next month. The gym now has your money, but it still owes you a month of training. Who owes whom? The gym owes you.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:30.567
At the year end, a business has four kinds of these amounts. Expenses owing and income received in advance are other payables: current liabilities. Expenses paid in advance and income still owing to us are other receivables: current assets.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:48.345
Here is the method. Is it an expense or income? Is it owing, or in advance? Then ask: who owes whom? If the business owes money or a service, it is an other payable. If the business is owed, it is an other receivable.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:01:01.670
You can also read a ledger balance. A debit balance brought down is an asset: a prepaid expense, or income owing. A credit balance is a liability: an expense owing, or income received in advance.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:13.270
For example, a rent receivable account with a credit balance brought down of seven hundred and fifty dollars means rent was received in advance. The business owes the tenant, so it is a liability.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:23.217
Here is a real question from June 2025. Which is a liability? One mark.

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:34.079
A and B are about irrecoverable debts. A debt recovered is income. A debt written off is an expense. Neither is something we owe.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:51.847
C: the tenant has paid rent for future months, so the business owes the tenant the use of the property. That is an other payable, a liability. D is the opposite. The business paid in advance, so it is owed. That is an asset.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.751
So the answer is C. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:10.425
A common mistake is calling income received in advance an asset, because the cash is in the bank. But the business still owes the service, so it is a liability.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.699
Another is a definition with no timing. Always say it is at the end of the financial year.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:32.969
Now you try. Leila's year ends on 31 March 2026. Classify each item as an other receivable or an other payable. Pause and decide.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:51.718
The insurance is prepaid, so the insurer owes Leila cover: a receivable. The commission is owed to her: a receivable. She owes the electricity: a payable. And she owes the tenant a month's use: a payable.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:59.384
Remember: expense or income, owing or in advance, then who owes whom. Now try the practice questions.
